  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through next Friday)\nIssued at 101 PM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nLate this weekend and early next week, the mid-level ridging is \nforecast to weaken/buckle on it's western flank with some bits of \nshortwave energy (vorticity) advancing through portions of West \nTexas and the Texas panhandle. This along with just enough of \nsufficient area moisture may help generate small (10-20%) chances \nfor rain, primarily focused in the Hill Country/Southern Edwards \nPlateau to areas along the Rio Grande. Guidance then shows the \nridging will once again strengthen across the region from mid to \nlate week, which likely is to help to suppress the rain chances \nacross the region through that time frame.\n\nDespite slightly weaker 500 mb heights on Sunday into early next \nweek, area temperatures don't look to budge much and stay above \naverage. Afternoon highs are to persistently hold in the mid to \nupper 90s for most while a few locations occasionally reach up \ntowards the triple digit mark. Heat indices are to run around or \nslightly above the ambient temperature, but peak values should \nremain shy of the heat advisory criteria with enough sufficient \nmixing each afternoon. The overnights are to remain persistently \nwarm as well, with most locations seeing the morning lows hold \nwithin the low to mid 70s. The Hill Country may be the slight \nexception where a few more upper 60 readings look to trickle in \nthrough mid to late next week.",
